home-library-failover for Debian and Ubuntu
===========================================

Two nodes run home-library. Exactly one of them -- the active node -- serves
the library and accepts changes; the other is a standby with a copy. A
handoff moves the SQLite database and the covers directory together, and
the active node advertises a floating address over Tailscale.

Installing this package changes nothing. A lone home-library keeps working
until `home-library-failover enable` is run.

How it works
------------

  home-library-db.service    at boot, before the app: works out which node
                             owns the database and pulls it here if this
                             node is taking over. At shutdown, after the app
                             has stopped: hands the database to the peer.
  home-library-vip.service   at boot, after the db unit: starts the app's
                             socket, waits for /library/healthz, then claims
                             the floating address. At shutdown: withdraws it.
  home-library-db-sync.timer hourly: the active node copies its database and
                             covers to the peer's /var/lib/home-library/standby.

`enable` also installs two drop-ins that make home-library.service and
home-library.socket conditional on /run/home-library-active, the flag the db
unit creates only on the node that should serve. A standby therefore never
starts the app, even if a request reaches its socket.

Which copy counts is recorded in /var/lib/home-library/OWNER as
"<node> <generation>", identical on both nodes. The generation rises on
every handoff, and a copy is never written over one a later handoff
produced. A node whose peer is unreachable keeps serving only if it already
owns the database; it never promotes itself on a guess.

PREFERRED names the node that reclaims the library when it boots. The other
node comes up active only if it owns the database already, i.e. the
preferred node handed over and has not come back.

One-time setup, on BOTH nodes
-----------------------------

1. Settings. In /etc/default/home-library-failover set PEER to the other
   node and PREFERRED to the same node on both. VIP must match on both.

2. Keys. Each node has two root-only keys, /root/id_homelibrary (handoffs)
   and /root/id_homelibrary_sync (hourly copy):

     ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' -f /root/id_homelibrary -C "home-library failover $(hostname -s)"
     ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N '' -f /root/id_homelibrary_sync -C "home-library-db-sync@$(hostname -s)"

   and the OTHER node's two public keys go in this node's
   /root/.ssh/authorized_keys, pinned:

     restrict,command="/usr/sbin/home-library-failover-peer" ssh-ed25519 AAAA... home-library failover <peer>
     restrict,command="/usr/bin/rrsync -wo /var/lib/home-library/standby" ssh-ed25519 AAAA... home-library-db-sync@<peer>

   sshd must permit root logins by key ("PermitRootLogin prohibit-password").

3. Tailscale. Add the VIP route to autoApprovers in the tailnet policy file;
   approving it by hand approves it for one node only. Clients that should
   reach the VIP need `tailscale set --accept-routes`.

4. Web server. Both nodes' sites `include snippets/home-library.conf;` and
   should answer on the VIP as well as on their own names.

5. Turn it on, on both nodes:

     home-library-failover enable

   Then run `home-library-failover to-local` on the node that should serve
   now, or reboot the preferred node.

Daily use
---------

  home-library-failover status      who owns it, and what runs where
  home-library-failover to-remote   hand the library to the peer now
  home-library-failover to-local    take it over here now

A planned reboot of the active node hands over by itself. If the active node
dies without shutting down, the standby will NOT take over on its own. To
serve the standby's copy -- which may lack the last hour of changes -- run,
on the standby:

  FORCE=1 home-library-failover to-local

When the other node comes back it sees the higher generation and pulls this
copy rather than pushing its own.

Before every replacement the receiving node snapshots its database to
library.db.<UTC time>Z beside it, keeping the newest ten. The hourly copy in
/var/lib/home-library/standby is a disaster copy: restoring it is a manual
step.

Turning it off
--------------

  home-library-failover disable

removes the drop-ins and disables the units, so this node runs home-library
on its own again. Do it on both nodes, and decide first which one keeps the
library.
